# N‐methyl‐2‐pyrrolidone Iodide as Functional Precursor Additive for Record Efficiency 2D Ruddlesden‐Popper (PEA)2(Cs)n−1PbnI3n+1 Solar Cells

## abstract
2D perovskite (PEA)2(Cs)n−1PbnI3n+1 (PEA: phenylethylammonium) exhibits more strengthened phase stability than its 3D components under ambient conditions and hence gained great attention in recent years. However, uncontrollable crystallization kinetics in (PEA)2(Cs)n−1PbnI3n+1 leads to difficulty in controlling film morphology and phase‐orientation regulation, resulting in poor power conversion efficiency (PCE). Herein, by incorporating precursor additive N‐methyl‐2‐pyrrolidone iodide (NMPI), the crystallization rate during the deposition of (PEA)2(Cs)n−1PbnI3n+1 film is efficiently regulated. As a result, the 2D or quasi‐2D perovskite solar cell (PSC) delivers record PCEs in all reported 2D or quasi‐2D CsPbX3 families, for instance, the quasi‐2D (n = 20) CsPbI3 PSC exhibits a record PCE of 14.59%, showing significantly enhanced stability. Detailed characterization reveals that the NMPI forms hydrogen bonds with dimethylammonium iodide (DMAI) in the precursor to control crystallization rate for a smooth morphology with small fluctuation, which leads to improved carrier lifetime and reduced trap‐density. More importantly, femtosecond transient absorption (fs‐TA) measurements confirm an improved phase purity and the suppressed nonradiative recombination in quasi‐2D perovskite film. It is believed that this simple additive strategy paves a new route for solving phase transition and crystallization kinetic problems in 2D and quasi‐2D CsPbX3.

N-methyl-2-pyrrolidone Iodide as Functional Precursor Additive for Record Efficiency 2D Ruddlesden-Popper $(\mathsf{P E A})_{2}(\mathsf{C s})_{n-1}\mathsf{P b}_{n}|_{3n+1}$ Solar Cells
2D perovskite $(\mathsf{P E A})_{2}(\mathsf{C s})_{n-1}\mathsf{P b}_{n}\mathsf{I}_{3n+1}$ (PEA: phenylethylammonium) exhibits more strengthened phase stability than its 3D components under ambient conditions and hence gained great attention in recent years. However, uncontrollable crystallization kinetics in $(\mathsf{P E A})_{2}(\mathsf{C s})_{n-1}\mathsf{P b}_{n}\mathsf{I}_{3n+1}$ leads to difficulty in controlling film morphology and phase-orientation regulation, resulting in poor power conversion efficiency (PCE). Herein, by incorporating precursor additive $N$ -methyl-2-pyrrolidone iodide (NMPI), the crystallization rate during the deposition of $(\mathsf{P E A})_{2}(\mathsf{C s})_{n-1}\mathsf{P b}_{n}\mathsf{I}_{3n+1}$ film is efficiently regulated. As a result, the 2D or quasi-2D perovskite solar cell (PSC) delivers record PCEs in all reported 2D or quasi-2D $\mathsf{c s P b}\mathsf{x}_{3}$ families, for instance, the quasi-2D $(n=20)$ $\mathsf{c s P b l}_{3}$ PSC exhibits a record PCE of $14.59\%$ , showing significantly enhanced stability. Detailed characterization reveals that the NMPI forms hydrogen bonds with dimethylammonium iodide (DMAI) in the precursor to control crystallization rate for a smooth morphology with small fluctuation, which leads to improved carrier lifetime and reduced trap-density. More importantly, femtosecond transient absorption (fs-TA) measurements confirm an improved phase purity and the suppressed nonradiative recombination in quasi-2D perovskite film. It is believed that this simple additive strategy paves a new route for solving phase transition and crystallization kinetic problems in 2D and quasi-2D $\mathsf{C s P b}\mathsf{X}_{3}$ .
## 1. Introduction
$\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}$ inorganic perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have emerged as burgeoning light harvesters and gained widespread attention by virtue of the highest power conversion efficiency (PCE) beyond $20\%$ for their outstanding photophysical properties, including superior thermal stability, suitable bandgap, and high carrier mobility.[1–4] Unfortunately, the phase transition problem, caused by the mismatched radius between ${\mathrm{Cs^{+}}}$ , $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , and $X^{-}$ ions, is a notable obstacle to block their potential commercialization.[5,6] Recently, numerous efforts have been made to increase their phase stability through ionic incorporation, steric hindrance, metastable phases and reducing dimension.[7–11] All of these strategies are well developed, ionic incorporation adjusts tolerance factor $(t)$ , and steric hindrance prevents $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ from distorting the corner-sharing octahedral framework.[9] Metastable phases $({\boldsymbol{\beta}},$ $\varkappa\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}$ ), especially $\gamma{\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}}$ show the most stable structure for its lowest dissociation energy than other phases.[12–14]
Moreover, one of the effective approaches to pursue superior phase stability is reducing dimension by inserting a bulky A-site (e.g., phenylethylammonium $(\mathrm{PEA^{+}})$ , $n$ -butylamine $(\mathrm{BA^{+}}))$ organic cation to form twodimensional (2D, $n\leq5$ ) or quasi-2D $(n>5)$ $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}$ perovskite.[15,16] Compared with 3D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}$ , the 2D or quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}$ shows a unique multiple quantum well structure, which typically induces steric hindrance effect, thereby improving phase stability against components decomposition, phase transition and oxidization.[16–22]
In 2016, Liao et al. first raised 2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}$ and researched its phase-orientation problem by introducing a large $\mathrm{BA}^{+}$ cation into the hydroiodic acid (HI)-containing precursor to induce a strong competition between $\mathrm{BA}^{+}$ and ${\mathrm{Cs^{+}}}$ cation. Although the growth orientation of 2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}$ was adjusted along the vertical direction on the substrate, the PSC with a poor PCE of $4.84\%$ was obtained.[23] In general, the performance of 2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}$ is poor, and it is difficult to achieve its increase in 2D or quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}$ PSCs. One of the limitations of its performance is poor arrangement in an in-plane orientation and irregular phases from the bottom to up, which impedes the outof-plane transport of carriers due to the low-conducting organic spacer layers.[24–26] Meanwhile, the uncontrollable crystallization kinetics during the spin-coating process induces a poor quality morphology, accompanied by abundant grain boundaries and defects.[27,28]
Therefore, all the recent work has focused on the two limitations. For example, Liu et  al. explored the different aromatic compounds with different ionic radii in the HI-containing precursor, including phenmethylammonium iodide (PMAI) and PEAI, to prepare quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}$ film with high-quality morphology at a low temperature. And an improved PCE of $5.7\%$ was obtained.[29] Zhao et  al. adopted a phase-arrangement strategy that ingeniously introduced a small amount of ethylenediamine (EDA) cation into $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ · $x\mathrm{HI}$ -containing precursors to induce a 2D $\mathrm{EDAPbI}_{4}$ formation, which largely improved the stability of $\alpha{\mathrm{-CsPbI}}_{3}$ with a highly reproducible PCE of $11.8\%$ .[30] Then, Chen et  al. further utilized the highly soluble precursor pair of cesium acetate (CsAc) and hydrogen lead trihalide $\left(\mathrm{HPb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}\right)$ to form strong interaction, which resulted in ultrasmooth and high-quality quasi-2D ( $n=60$ ) $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ film with a high PCE of $12.4\%$ .[31] Recently, our group utilized $\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}$ to obtain highquality $\gamma{\mathrm{Cs}}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ and introduced a judicious amount of PEAI to adjust the dimensionality of $\gamma{\mathrm{Cs}}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ from 2D to quasi-2D and 3D. Also, their carrier kinetics were detailedly researched to boost the performance of quasi-2D $[n=40]$ ) $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ to $13.65\%$ . However, despite the fact that the highest PCE of $13.65\%$ is obtained in quasi-2D $(n=40)\mathrm{CsPbI_{3}}$ , it is still far behind its 3D components.[1,32] Meanwhile, the stability and PCE of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}$ should also be further improved in a smaller dimension $(n<40)$ . Therefore, great efforts should be made to conquer these limitations and explore intrinsic mechanism of 2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}$ , which is meaningful for further boosting their performances.
In this work, $N$ -methyl-2-pyrrolidone iodide (NMPI) was synthesized as a functional precursor additive to improve the phase stability and photophysical properties of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ . Based on the typical precursor of HI hydrolysis-derived intermediates dimethylammonium iodide (DMAI) in 2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}$ , we found that the hydrogen bond between NMPI additive and DMAI could control the crystallization rate during the deposition of $(\mathrm{PEA})_{2}(\mathrm{Cs})_{n-1}\mathrm{Pb}_{n}\mathrm{I}_{3n+1},$ thereby obtaining a high-quality $(\mathrm{PEA})_{2}(\mathrm{Cs})_{n-1}\mathrm{Pb}_{n}\mathrm{I}_{3n+1}$ film with smooth morphology, few pinholes, and low defect density. Upon optimization, the performance of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ in different dimensions from 2D, quasi-2D, and 3D was largely improved, and the corresponding device also showed strengthened stability.
## 2. Result and Discussion
In this work, we have successfully synthesized the NMPI compounds through a simple reaction of N-methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP) (detailed synthesis procedure listed in the Supporting Information). Figure  1a displays the molecular structure of $\mathrm{DMA}^{+}$ and ${\mathrm{NMP^{+}}}$ cations, both of which belong to organic groups.[9,33] To further deeply characterize the properties of the powders, scanning electron microscope (SEM), energy-dispersive spectrometric (EDS), and X-ray diffraction (XRD) are utilized to study their components and morphology properties. The XRD patterns are listed in Figure S1 (Supporting Information), and inserted with compound images of DMAI and NMPI, respectively. Then, SEM is used to characterize the morphology and elemental composition of NMPI. As shown in Figure 1b,c, the NMPI presents a rod-like structure in general, which is stacked in small sheets. Moreover, the EDS mapping of Figure $\mathrm{1d{-}g}$ shows that C, N, and O are distributed uniformly throughout the NMPI powder. Furthermore, the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) of NMPI, PEAI, and DMAI has performed in nitrogen as shown in Figure S2a (Supporting Information). The sublimation of NMPI powders starts at ${\approx}145~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and completely disappears at ${\approx}200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which is earlier than DMAI $(\approx210~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . This indicates that NMPI is removed during the $210^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ annealing. Besides, we compared the reduced mass (Δmass) of perovskite precursor powders (without solvents and annealing process) and the scraped perovskite film in Figure S2b and Table S1 (Supporting Information). Because the perovskite films were annealed in a humidity environment $(\mathrm{RH}={\approx}40\%)$ , and moisture can promote the release of DMAI additive.[34,35] We found that there were none NMPI remained in both scraped perovskite films, and only ${\approx}6.5\%$ and ${\approx}7.2\%$ DMAI remained in reference $10\%$ NMPI) and optimized $2\%$ NMPI) film, respectively. Differently, the perovskite precursor powders showed ${\approx}24.4\%$ DMAI and ${\approx}5.4\%$ NMPI remained. The remained $\mathrm{DMA}^{+}$ in perovskite film could be aggregated on the grain boundary, and only a small number of them doping into the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ as previously reported.[35,36]
To further explore the interaction in the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ precursor, nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) and Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra are conducted to discuss the interaction in each component. In Figure  1h, the NMR spectrum of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ precursors is presented, including DMAI, NMPI, $\mathrm{NMPI}{\cdot}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , and NMPI-DAMI powder, which is measured by dissolving them in dimethyl sulfoxide-d6 (DMSO-d6). The DMAI shows signals of ${\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}^{+}{\mathrm{-}}$ (singlet) and ${\mathrm{-CH}}_{3}$ (doublet) protons, which are located at $\delta=8.17$ ppm and $\delta=2.53$ ppm, respectively. Moreover, the ratio of the signals of ${\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}^{+}{\mathrm{-}}$ and ${\mathrm{-CH}}_{3}$ after integration is 1:3.08, and corresponding to previous reports.[33] Differently, NMPI shows several peaks in a small chemical shift $(1.5{-}3.5\mathrm{ppm}_{.}$ , and all of the integrating peaks are corresponding well with its molecular structure, indicating that NMPI originates from the reaction of NMP and HI solution. Then, we individually add $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and DMAI into NMPI, and find that the NMR spectrums of NMPI and $\mathrm{NMPI}{\cdot}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ show similar peaks, but the chemical shift for active hydrogen of NMPI in NMPI $({\approx}9.40\ \mathrm{ppm})$ and NMPI- $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ $(\approx5.03\mathrm{\ppm})$ is different. Moreover, the chemical shift of $\mathrm{NMPI}{\cdot}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ has a small shift compared to pure NMPI in Figure 1i, which shows the existence of small interaction between NMPI and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ .[37,38] For NMPIDMAI and DMAI powders, the chemical shift is enlarged in Figure 1i,j. We find that the peak of ${\mathrm{-CH}}_{3}$ in DMAI is split into three small peaks between 2.45 ppm and $2.55\mathrm{ppm}$ , and ${\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}^{+}$ shifts toward low chemical shift after NMPI addition. This could be ascribed to the formation of a hydrogen bond $(\mathrm{N-H\cdotsO})$ between NMPI and DMAI, which retards the rapid exchange of active hydrogen in DMAI.[39–41] Besides, the other NMR spectrums of NMPI-CsI and NMPI-PEAI are also shown in Figure S3 (Supporting Information). From the slight shift in NMPI-PEAI, we deduce that there is also a reaction appearing between NMPI and the bulky A-site cation $(\mathrm{PEA^{+}})$ , which may work together to retard the rapid crystallization. Subsequently, FT-IR spectra are used to further confirm the interaction appearance between the NMPI and DMAI additives during the precursor, as shown in Figure  1k. It can be observed that NMPI largely influences the DMAI. Moreover, according to the enlarged FT-IR spectra of $\mathrm{C-H}$ stretching vibration and $\mathsf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{H}$ bending vibration in Figure $^{11,\mathrm{m}}$ , both of them shift to high wavenumber after the introduction of NMPI into DMAI, confirming the interaction forms between DMAI and NMPI.[4,40]
Subsequently, we systematically synthesized the low-dimensional $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ with chemical formula as $(\mathrm{PEA})_{2}(\mathrm{Cs})_{n-1}\mathrm{Pb}_{n}\mathrm{I}_{3n+1}$ (simplified as 2D/quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathrm{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3})$ by deliberately introducing bulky organic cations of $\mathrm{PEA^{+}}$ . Notably, the quasi-2D $(n=20)$ $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ is taken as an example for research in this work. To elucidate the influence of NMPI additive in morphology and device performances, top-view atomic force microscopy (AFM), SEM, and contact angle are adopted to reveal the function of NMPI additive in morphology and wettability of quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ . From Figure 2a, the morphology of quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ is constituted with anomalous flake-shaped film, which is similar to the early reports of quasi-2D perovskite.[32] With the concentration of NMPI (relative to DMAI) increasing from $0\%$ to $2\%$ and final $3\%$ , the roughness of quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ fluctuates from $26.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ to $19.9\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and finally to $25.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The topography with small fluctuation in an optimal ratio of $2\%$ is favorable to better contact between the perovskite layer and hole-transport layer (HTL), and has a small leakage current and reduced nonradiative losses. Similarly, in Figure 2b, the morphology of $2\%$ NMPI additive quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ (named as optimal) films tends to be more dense and uniform with fewer pinholes formed. Notably, the introduction of too much NMPI $3\%$ NMPI) deteriorates quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ morphology with high roughness and large holes, this may be attributed to too low crystallization rate originate from a variety of interactions, and result in poor coverage.[42] Importantly, morphology with fewer pinholes is beneficial to prevent humidity erosion, which may be attributed to the formation of hydrogen bond in NMPI and DMAI that delays the quick crystallization rate and improves perovskite quality during the deposition of the perovskite film.[40] It is also proved by Figure  2c, according to Wenzel's model, the smallest roughness $(19.9\ \mathrm{nm})$ of the optimal film results in an improved contact angle of $69.8^{\circ}$ , indicating an increase in moisture resistance.[43,44]
XRD patterns are taken to evaluate the influence of NMPI additive on the crystal structure of the final quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ films. Figure  3a shows that there is no structural difference in all quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ films with different concentrations of NMPI additives, and all of them show two typical peaks of (111) and (202) plane at $2\theta=\approx14^{\circ}$ and $29^{\circ}$ , respectively.[45] It points that NMPI does not change the structure of perovskite, instead, it mainly just influences the crystallization rate during the perovskite formation. Notably, in Figure 3b, with the increased concentration of NMPI additive from $0\%$ to $0.5\%$ , the (202) plane shifts toward a smaller $2\theta_{\mathrm{i}}$ indicating an expansion of the interlayer spacing. However, the (202) plane shows unchanged when the concentration of NMPI additive is further increased. This phenomenon could be the addition of NMPI retards the evaporation of DMAI and the more remnant of $\mathrm{DMA}^{+}$ intercalating into the perovskite crystal but the amount of doping $\mathrm{DMA}^{+}$ showed an upper limit. This could be proved by the crystallization process in Figure S4 (Supporting Information). The optimal film with NMPI introduction shows a slow crystallization rate than the reference one during the early stage of the annealing process.[33,36] Figure S5 (Supporting Information) is conducted to further characterize the crystallization process of quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ films. It can be seen that as the annealing time increases, the perovskite film first appears two typical peaks as discussed above, and the peaks at $2\theta=12.8^{\circ}$ emerge as a $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ peak after $18\mathrm{min}$ of annealing, indicating that quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ film decomposes.[31] There is a small shift in XRD during $10{-}14~\mathrm{min}$ annealing, which may be caused by the decomposition of perovskite film with the change of crystal structure. Besides, a new peak that highlights orange appears during the quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ formation and disappears after $10\ \mathrm{min}$ of annealing. It may be ascribed to the appearance of DMAI- $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ intermediate, and the disappearance after $10\ \mathrm{min}$ annealing may be due to the DMAI sublimation at $210{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.{}^{[34]}$ This can be verified by the NMR spectra of evaporating (volatile) powders of pristine ( $0\%$ NMPI) and optimized $2\%$ NMPI) in Figure S6 (Supporting Information), there are hardly observed $\mathrm{PEA}^{+}$ peaks, instead, $\mathrm{DMA}^{+}$ peaks can be vividly observed.
FT-IR spectra of quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ powders scraped from the glass substrate is measured to further confirm the influence of NMPI additive, as shown in Figure 3c. From the enlarged figure of Figure  3d, the $_\mathrm{N-H}$ stretching vibration of the quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ shifts to a higher wavenumber $(3437-3459~\mathrm{cm^{-1}})$ with the increased concentration of NMPI additive. This tendency can indicate the interaction appearing between NMPI and residual DAMI in quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ , which could be possibly ascribed into a small amount of NMPI retrains the evaporation of DMAI and the more remnant of $\mathrm{DMA}^{+}$ .[46,47] This could be verified by the results of TGA as we discussed above. Meanwhile, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) is used to characterize the surface chemical states of quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ films after the introduction of NMPI additive, as shown in Figure  3e. Compared with the pristine film, the peaks of Cs 3d, Pb 4f and I 3d5 all shift toward lower binding energies as the concentration of NMPI additive increases (Figure $\mathtt{S7a,c}$ Supporting Information). These tendencies could originate from the increase in the amount of NMPI, and induce residual $\mathrm{DMA}^{+}$ doping into $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ because of greater electronegativity of pseudo-alkali metals $(\mathrm{DMA^{+}})$ than ${\mathrm{Cs^{+}}}$ .[48] Notably, N 1s peak cannot be observed in perovskite film (Figure 3f), while appears in the fresh film without solvents (Figure S8, Supporting Information). This difference mainly originates that the perovskite films are treated with a slight surface etching to obtain a fresh sample surface prior to perovskite testing, which would remove the low content of residual organic molecules. Moreover, another probable reason is the good phase distribution with small- $\cdot n$ perovskite phase rich at the bottom close to the substrate side, and the large- $\cdot n$ phases rich at the top surface of the perovskite film.[49–51]
Subsequently, the optoelectronic properties of quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ after NMPI additive are investigated through UV–vis spectra, the steady-state photoluminescence (PL) and timeresolution photoluminescence (TRPL) spectrum. As shown in Figure 3g, the absorption edge for the quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ shows a slight red-shift as the NMPI additive increases. Thus, the corresponding bandgap shows a decreasing tendency in Figure $\mathrm{S9a}$ (Supporting Information). The redshift is mainly ascribed to the doping of a small number of $\mathrm{DMA}^{+}$ organic cations into the crystal lattice as we discussed above.[33,52] Then, the PL and TRPL are conducted in a structure of glass/perovskite for the carrier's behavior of quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ . As shown in Figure 3h, the PL intensity of $2\%$ NMPI quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ film is enhanced compared with the pristine film, which indicates that the defect states are reduced after the introduction of NMPI additive due to the formation of high-quality quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ films.[52] Figure S9b (Supporting Information) shows the decay curves with a three-exponential function applied to fit it. The calculated carrier lifetimes $\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}$ is listed in Table S2 (Supporting Information), where the carrier lifetimes are improved in optimal quasi2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ film. These results further confirm the conclusion of PL spectrum that NMPI could retard the rapid crystallization rate to form a uniform morphology with fewer trap states and suppressed recombination.[8,53]
Subsequently, the femtosecond transient absorption (fsTA) measurements are conducted to study the photoinduced charge-transfer dynamics of quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ , which is applied under a $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser pump excitation. Figure  4a,d shows the 3D plot TA spectrograms of quasi-2D $[n=20]$ ) $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ , which is a function of delay time and wavelength. There are two signals of negative (blue) ground-state bleach (GSB) and positive (yellow) excited-state absorption (ESA). For the pristine quasi2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ film, the positive band in the short-wavelength region of ${\approx}500\ \mathrm{nm}$ belongs to ESA of the low-dimensional phase, while it is not obvious in the optimal film, indicating that NMPI additive could improve the phase purity. Similar results are shown in Figure S10a,d (Supporting Information) of another quasi-2D $(n=40)$ $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ .[54] The bleach signals appear near $700\mathrm{nm}$ for both films, only a slight red-shift occurring on the optimal film. Notably, the photo-induced absorption change (ΔA) of GSB for the pristine quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ film gradually decreases with the prolonged delay time, suggesting strengthened recombination between the photo-induced electrons and holes.[54] Differently, the GSB peak intensity is much stronger for the optimal films for the same delay time, which is ascribed to the lower carrier recombination rate and reduced trap densities after the introduction of NMPI additive.[55] Besides, the TA spectra with different time scales of charge carrier dynamics is shown in Figure  4b,e. After 5  ns, the bleaching signals of the pristine quasi-2D $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}$ film have almost completely decayed, while the optimal one shows obvious signals, indicating a longer decay time of carriers after the introduction of NMPI additive. Besides, a red-shift happens in both quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ as the decay time increases, which may be an indication of exciton dissociation or thermalization. It also happens on quasi-2D $[n=40]$ $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ films in Figure S10b,e (Supporting Information).[56] Moreover, compared with the pristine quasi2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ film, the optimal film shows a more uniform and smoother peak intensity, which further confirms that the NMPI additive could help to improve the purity of low-dimensional phases.[32]
Further, the TA dynamics probed for pristine and optimal quasi-2D ( $n=20^{\cdot}$ ) $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ film is shown in Figure  4c,f, which fits with a three-exponential decay equation. The ultrafast decay time components, $\tau_{1}$ and $\tau_{2}$ , are used to elaborate the process of photogenerated carrier being extracted out from the active layer, while the slow decay time $(\tau_{3})$ is adopted to interpret the process of excited-state decay or trap-assisted recombination in bulk perovskite and all interfaces.[57] Although carrier extraction time shows of the pristine quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ film (725 fs to 26.8 ps) is faster than that of the optimal one (2–204 ps), the $\tau_{3}$ of the latter is 10-fold longer than the former (3180 vs 289 ps), as depicted in Scheme  1. The slow extracted time of optimal quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ may originate from the increased amount of $\mathrm{DMA}^{+}$ doped into quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3},$ and influences its photophysical properties.[9,58] Importantly, the prolonged $\tau_{3}$ shows that the NMPI additive optimizes the crystallization kinetics of quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ , thereby greatly suppressing the interfacial recombination and current leakage, and resulting in improved morphology and phase purity.[59,60] The conclusion is also confirmed by quasi-2D $[n=40]$ ) $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ in Figure S10c,f (Supporting Information).
Finally, a device structure with the cross-sectional SEM of glass substrate/fluorine-doped tin oxide $\mathrm{(FTO)}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ quasi-2D $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}/2,2,7,7^{\prime}$ -tetrakis (N,N-di-pmethoxyphenylamine)- $^{9,9^{\prime}}$ - spirobifluorene (Spiro-OMeTAD)/Au is used to evaluate the photovoltaic performance, as shown in Figure S11 (Supporting Information). The thickness of quasi-2D $(n=20)$ $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ is almost $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which ensures efficient absorption of the incident light. As shown in Figure 5a and Table 1, the current density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves of the optimal device present that the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ , FF, $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ , and PCE are respectively 1.08  V (1.05  V), $67.13\%$ $(67.43\%)$ , $19.95\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ $(19.97\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ , and $14.59\%$ $(14.13\%)$ under reverse (forward) voltage scan, which is the highest PCE compared with those previously reported in quasi-2D $(n=40)$ )
$\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ .[32] Besides, the small $J{-}V$ hysteresis may originate from the improved quality of perovskite because of the NMPI additive. The external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra shown in Figure  5b presents a broad absorbance over the range of $300{-}800\ \mathrm{nm}$ with an integration $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $19.96\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ from the EQE curve under AM 1.5G illumination conditions, which agrees well with the value in $J{-}V$ characterization. Then, the stabilized efficiency is tested at a bias of the maximum power point (0.88 V) under continuous AM 1.5G illumination. The optimal device shows a steady-state photocurrent of $16.14\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ with a stabilized PCE of $14.20\%$ in Figure 5c.
To further understand the effects of NMPI additive in trap-state densities, the space charge limited current (SCLC) is conducted by a device structure of $\mathrm{FTO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2/}$ quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}_{3}}/[6,6]$ -phenyl C61 butyric acid methyl ester $({\mathrm{PCBM}})/$ Ag and FTO/Poly[bis(4-phenyl) (2,4,6-trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA)/quasi-2D $\mathrm{CsPbI_{3}/S p i r o/A g}$ for electron-only (Figure S12a,b, Supporting Information) and hole-only (Figure S12c,d, Supporting Information), respectively. There are three regions with different slope values $(k)$ , including a linear region (ohmic region) of voltage versus current $(k=1)$ at low bias, a trap-filled region at an intermediated region $(k>3)$ , and a trap-free SCLC region (child region) at high bias $(k=2)$ .[61,62] Notably, the trap-filled limited voltage $(V_{\mathrm{TFL}})$ is defined as a kink point between the ohmic region and the trap-filled region. According to the trap-state densities formula of $N_{\mathrm{t}}=$ $(2\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}\varepsilon_{0}V_{\mathrm{TFL}})/q L^{2}$ , where $\varepsilon_{0},\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}},q$ , and $L$ present the vacuum permittivity, the relative dielectric constant, elementary charge, and thickness of perovskite film, respectively.[63] Therefore, the corresponding $N_{\mathrm{t}}$ is calculated as $6.64\times10^{15}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ (1.71 $\times~10^{15}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ and $4.01\times10^{15}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ $(1.58\times10^{15}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ for pristine and optimal devices in electron-only (hole-only), respectively. The lower $N_{\mathrm{t}}$ values could contribute to the function of the NMPI additive as we discussed above, which results in a reduced charge carrier recombination, improved mobility and enhanced device performances.[45]
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, the NMPI additive is successfully developed for the first time to control the crystallization kinetics during $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ formation by reaction with DMAI through hydrogen bond $(\mathrm{N-H\cdotsO})$ . Systematical analyses show that NMPI additive achieves uniform morphology with few pinholes, reduced trap densities, and improved purity of low-dimension phases. These improvements further lead to enlarged carrier lifetime and suppressed nonradiative. As a result, the PCEs in 2D or quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ are largely improved to record ones. Moreover, a record PCE for quasi-2D $(n=20)$ ) $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ of $14.59\%$ is obtained with negligible hysteresis, which is the highest PCE so far among the reported low-dimensional $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}$ . Meanwhile, the optimal quasi-2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ PSC could maintain higher stability than the reference. In short, this work presents a novel strategy through additive regulation of crystallization kinetics in 2D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}$ to overcome the phase transition problem, and pursues a more efficient and stable optoelectronic device in the future.
